  "query_preview": "..Troughing strengthens across much of the West Coast \ntoday as ridging weakens somewhat across the southwest today \nenabling another monsoonal moisture push this afternoon. This will \nresult in a flash flood threat across southwestern UT and portions \nof central UT this afternoon, as well as a severe weather threat \nacross northern UT, western UT, and extreme southwestern UT. \nFollowing todays threat, conditions look to dry out through the \nweekend, potentially into midweek next week. \n\nAn H5 trough situated over the PNW continues to deepen this \nafternoon with multiple shortwave perturbations progressing SW to NE \nacross the great basin will provide ascent for multiple rounds of \nscattered showers and thunderstorms this afternoon across UT. \nCurrently across southwestern UT, leftover subsidence from earlier \nthunderstorms this morning has resulted in a convective lull which \nis expected to last through around noon-2pm MT this afternoon as a \ndeepening shortwave pushes into the state with flow enhancing aloft. \nThe flooding threat is forecast to continue to increase with these \nstorms through the afternoon as PWATs continue to increase, \ncharacterized by values in excess of 1.2\", perhaps higher as CAM \nguidance has not handled this well today (i.e. recent Dugway \nsounding characterized by 1.3\" PWATs in the West Desert), indicating \nthat values may actually be higher across SW UT. Additionally, \ntraining storms may result in rather high rainfall rates reaching \nand/or exceeding 0.5\"/hr. This raises concern for recent burn scars, \nsuch as Cottonwood, Forsyth, Monroe Canyon, France Canyon, Silver \nKing, and the new Widemouth 2 burn scar. \n\nFurther north, showers are ongoing across much of northern UT \nspurred by WAA in the midlevels. These showers will continue to \ndissipate through the morning, with stronger storms expected to form \nafter 4pm MT as the shortwave impulse deepens with a 30kt H5 jet \ndeveloping overhead. Strong upper diffluence is forecast to develop \nwith perhaps multiple 50kt jet streaks developing overhead around \nthe time of CI, resulting in EBWD values from 30-40kts this \nafternoon supporting the potential for a couple supercells this \nafternoon. The primary threat with these thunderstorms are severe \nwind gusts in excess of 58mph, particularly if they can root \neffectively within the boundary layer this afternoon. Some \nuncertainty regarding this exists due to persistent cloud cover, \nthough diurnal heating with current cloud breaks supports some \nairmass recovery this afternoon in the midst of dewpoints in the \nupper 50s to low 60s. Lastly, this threat is enhanced as the early \nmorning convection has left behind a few different outflow \nboundaries, in particular north of the airport toward the bountiful \narea as well as another potentially closer to the ID border. This \nwill locally enhance vorticity, and with daytime heating expected to \nincrease MLCAPE to at least 150J/kg, support some stronger and \npotentially longer-lived storms developing. \n\nWith the ridge pushing east and dry air advecting in tomorrow with \nthe aforementioned trough pushing in, PoPs decrease somewhat \ntomorrow afternoon statewide. The flash flood threat exists across \nfar southwest UT once again as the moisture reservoir of higher \nPWATs remains in place, though precip will begin to favor higher \nterrain otherwise. Into this weekend through potentially midweek \nnext week, mid-level moisture will remain in place as the ridge \ncontinues sliding east favoring terrain-driven thunderstorms and a \nlower-elevation drying trend. Ensemble guidance continues to push \nback the true return to monsoonal moisture, potentially after \nWednesday next week.",
